#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128972 :question/id #uuid "6d363483-4ce5-5c17-8ab8-11bfcf8f9282" :question/math-academy-id "e-5888" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8d6f70aed4d013bfcdd9060124ad003e3e1ed6ce70852e4f60d71cdd8d65cc4.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x = -1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n\n- $x=-3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\n- $p(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x = -1$ has an even multiplicity because at $x = -1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement II is true. The roots $x = -3$ and $x=2$ are simple roots because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the points, but the curve isn't tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves left in the negative direction towards $-\\infty,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $\\infty.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96ec7d09-8bfa-5f1b-9f5b-87427654f0a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127670} {:answer/id #uuid "9a37ad05-69e2-5ecd-a7a6-3755791139ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127671} {:answer/id #uuid "fe99d6e7-e527-59ca-bd28-1d6682864a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127672} {:answer/id #uuid "4adb3ede-6d8c-5d6d-8697-c125203da4ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127673} {:answer/id #uuid "75d4d3d4-7bc6-542b-9bad-